   "result": {
    "conversation_id": "d135dd58-cf2f-4c55-a9f8-a8b29235f050",
    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel disappointed and to look for explanations after coming so close to securing a promotion—especially given your strong background, 10 years of service, and relevant degrees. \n\nHowever, **making an issue out of the panel’s gender composition at this stage is not advisable**, nor does the composition alone signify unfair bias. \n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with practical advice on how to handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition Does Not Prove Bias\n* **Role-based appointments:** In the civil service, selection panels are typically composed based on organizational roles (e.g., the hiring director, a peer division head, a subject-matter expert, and an HR/independent representative) rather than personal demographic design. If those senior posts happen to be held by women, an all-female panel will naturally result.\n* **The evidentiary bar:** Just as an all-male panel does not inherently prove bias against female candidates, an all-female panel does not demonstrate bias against male candidates. Making a claim or insinuation based solely on the demographic makeup of the panel is speculative and would likely be viewed by HR or senior leadership as an unfounded reaction to not getting the job.\n\n### 2. Relative Qualifications and Selection Criteria\n* **Shortlisting vs. Final Selection:** Making a final shortlist of three confirms that you were considered fully qualified on paper. At the interview stage, decisions in the public sector rarely come down to who has the most years on the job or a specific degree title.\n* **Strategic fit and interview performance:** A Master's in political science and direct experience leading international projects may have aligned more closely with the ministry's immediate strategic priorities for this specific role. Furthermore, public sector interviews typically score candidates against a structured competency framework (e.g., strategic leadership, stakeholder engagement, delivering at pace). A candidate can perform better on the day against those specific indicators regardless of background length.\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay for Feedback\n* Civil service HR processes are notoriously slow. Compiling, moderating, and agreeing on written feedback among four busy panel members often takes several weeks, especially if the recruitment campaign had multiple rounds or if post-interview administration is centralized. Two weeks without a response is common and not necessarily a cause for alarm.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up for feedback:**\n   Frame your request positively, focusing on professional development and alignment with civil service competency frameworks.\n   > *\"Dear [HR Contact / Panel Chair], I am following up on my request from two weeks ago regarding feedback from the recent Senior Policy Adviser interview. As I am keen to continue developing my career within the department, I would greatly appreciate any written notes or scores against the core competencies to help guide my future applications.\"*\n\n2. **Review the competency scoring sheet:**\n   In most civil service systems, interviewers must record numerical scores and written rationale against predetermined criteria. When you receive this feedback:\n   * Look at where the panel scored you lower.\n   * Review whether the feedback is objective, substantive, and tied directly to the published job specifications.\n\n3. **Decide if there is any genuine procedural issue:**\n   * If the feedback reveals clear procedural flaws, deviation from the merit principle, or objective evidence of discrimination, you can consult your union representative, employee association, or the relevant Civil Service Commission appeal process.\n   * If the feedback is standard, coherent, and reflects a legitimate scoring difference, the most productive path is to use those insights for the next senior competition.\n",
